  "content": {
    "id": "p2-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Consider the following sequence:\n$$\\begin{aligned}a_1&=1^2+2^2+2^2,\\\\a_2&=2^2+3^2+6^2,\\\\a_3&=3^2+4^2+(12)^2,\\\\a_4&=4^2+5^2+(20)^2.\\end{aligned}$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the first four terms of this sequence and show that each of these terms is a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9=3^2,\\quad49=7^2,\\quad169=13^2,\\quad441=21^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a_1&=1^2+2^2+2^2=9=3^2,\\\\a_2&=2^2+3^2+6^2=49=7^2,\\\\a_3&=3^2+4^2+12^2=169=13^2,\\\\a_4&=4^2+5^2+20^2=441=21^2.\\end{align*}$$\nEach term is the square of an integer.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write down the general term for this sequence.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a_n=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2$.",
          "worked": "The three squared quantities are $n$, $n+1$ and $n(n+1)$.\n$$a_n=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-continuation-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that every term in this sequence is a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a_n=(n^2+n+1)^2$.\n\nExpand the general term and factorise; $n^2+n+1$ is an integer.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a_n&=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2\\\\a_n&=n^2+n^2+2n+1+n^4+2n^3+n^2\\\\a_n&=n^4+2n^3+3n^2+2n+1\\\\a_n&=(n^2+n+1)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every positive integer $n$, $n^2+n+1$ is an integer, so every term is a perfect square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
